{"t":"The 2026 Landscape of Regenerative Aesthetics",
 "b":["Shift from volumetric correction to biological regeneration","Biostimulators and bioregulators now mainstream","Polynucleotides: fastest-growing injectable class in Europe & Asia","Exosomes: high interest, heavy hype, unresolved regulation","Biomimetic peptides: maturing from cosmeceutical to injectable science"],
 "n":"The field has pivoted from filling volume to instructing tissue regeneration. Polynucleotides are among the fastest growing injectable categories in Europe and Asia, while exosomes generate enormous interest alongside unresolved regulatory and quality concerns. Biomimetic peptides are transitioning from topical marketing toward genuine injectable pharmacology."},
{"t":"Paradigm Shift: From Filling to Instructing",
 "b":["Traditional fillers: passive, space-occupying, temporary","Biostimulators (PLLA, CaHA): scaffold + controlled collagenesis","New generation: instructive molecular signals — ECM chemical intelligence","Goal: restore tissue homeostasis, not just contour","Longevity framing: skin as a regenerating organ system"],
 "n":"Hyaluronic acid fillers are passive space occupiers; biostimulators add a scaffold and controlled collagenesis. The newest generation delivers instructive molecular signals that reprogram fibroblast behavior — the essence of chemical intelligence. In the longevity framework we treat skin and hair as regenerating organ systems, not surfaces."},
{"t":"Skin & Hair Longevity: A Unified Framework",
 "b":["Skin aging = ECM degradation + senescence + chronic inflammation","Hair miniaturization shares dermal papilla ECM dysfunction","Shared hallmarks: inflammaging, oxidative stress, niche decline","Injectable biologics target shared upstream drivers","Metrics: cutometry, dermal ultrasound, trichoscopy, PROMs"],
 "n":"Cutaneous aging and follicular miniaturization share upstream drivers: matrix degradation, senescent cell burden, oxidative stress, and low-grade inflammation. The dermal papilla matrix is as central to hair cycling as the dermal matrix is to skin quality — explaining why the same three injectable classes appear across both indications."},
{"t":"How to Read the Evidence: Grading in This Review",
 "b":["Level A: multiple RCTs / meta-analysis — practice-changing","Level B: single RCT or consistent split-face trials — supportive","Level C: observational / case series — hypothesis-generating","Level D: preclinical / mechanistic only — investigational","Each module closes with an evidence summary table"],
 "n":"Throughout, claims are graded on a four-level scale. Level A should change practice; Level B is supportive but preliminary; Level C generates hypotheses only; Level D is preclinical and must not be marketed as proven. Keep these grades in mind when counseling patients and when industry presents data."},
